# UI language — Hindi script, Latin digits

## Static labels (buttons, titles, hints, errors, accessibility)

- Add or change strings **only** in `src/i18n/ui_hi.ts` via the `UI` object (or small helpers exported next to it). Do **not** embed English/Hinglish literals in JSX for user-facing chrome.
- For new screens/components, import `UI` (and `rewardDisplay`, `skillUiLabel` when relevant) instead of typing copy inline.

## Dynamic / lesson-facing text

- Short Roman lesson tokens (options, chip labels, “correct answer” hints): use `formatLearningSurfaceText()` from `src/i18n/englishInDevanagari.ts`.
- Mapping step English side as Devanagari transliteration: `englishLessonLabelToDevanagari()`.
- **Any** string shown in the UI that might contain digits (server JSON, placement text, prompts, feedback body, analyses): wrap the **display** value with `toLatinDigits()` from the same module. Prefer applying at render time rather than changing TTS/audio payload unless product asks for parity.

## Do not

- Use Devanagari numerals (`०`-`९`) in UI; ASCII `0`-`9` only.
- Scatter one-off Hindi strings inside `*.tsx` — add a key under `UI` instead.
- Run `formatLearningSurfaceText` or aggressive transliteration on long narrative / TTS paragraphs meant to stay natural; normalize digits only (`toLatinDigits`) when those strings appear on screen.

## Backend content

- Prompts and fallbacks under `server/llm_content.py` should ask model output for Devanagari Hindi with **ASCII digits** for numbers (aligned with mixed-content TTS/STT prompts).
